    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Some reservoirs rely on fractures for permeability and the flow of hydrocarbons. For such reservoirs, information on fracture geometry azimuth and spatial distribution is important to understand the reservoir behavior and to optimize the production strategy.\r\n\r\nThere are two aspects to the characterization of fractured reservoirs (i) providing a deterministic geological description of the observed fractures  (seismically mapped faults and minor fractures encountered in cores or logs) and (ii) predicting the characteristics of fractures for the interwell space addressing sub-seismic-scale faults and joints.\r\n\r\nThis paper presents a methodology for such predictions based on geomechanical principles rather than statistical techniques. The method entails using analog outcrop data in combination with a boundary element model of the seismically mapped fault pattern.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
